@@ -0,0 +1,547 @@
+//! `lakelet-load --profile` — the mode that puts this process in the state you want to look at.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.16-profiling`. A sampler reports where a *process* spent its time, and that is
+//! evidence about a store only if the process was doing the store's work: so this mode drops the
+//! socket the ingest mode writes to and drives a store of its own, in this process, in a loop long
+//! enough to look at.
+//!
+//! One cycle is four steps, in the order a load run does them:
+//!
+//! 1. **the workload** — [`super::simulate`]'s records for one batch, with their timestamps moved
+//!    past every cycle before this one, so no two segments a run writes state the same span;
+//! 2. **the protocol path** — those records as the frames a client would send, read back out of
+//!    their own bytes, so the store holds what the wire carried rather than what the generator
+//!    meant;
+//! 3. **the flush** — one segment, sealed into the store;
+//! 4. **the question** — one query about the span this cycle just wrote, answered by the executor
+//!    `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` shipped and counted by the phases
+//!    `lakelet/4.15-budget-regression` established.
+//!
+//! The fourth step is what makes the loop evidence rather than load: the records a cycle ingests are
+//! the records its query reads and answers, so a run's phase counts are numbers a reader can check
+//! by hand. `decode == filter == aggregate == records` (no predicate was asked, and one window holds
+//! the whole of one cycle's question), `blocks == decompress == cycles` (one segment was read per
+//! cycle), and `headers` is one entry per segment per cycle — `cycles * (cycles + 1) / 2`.
+//!
+//! # Why there are two ways to stop
+//!
+//! `--cycles` stops after a number of cycles and `--seconds` stops after a wall-clock bound, and the
+//! difference between them is the whole reason both exist. `--cycles` is the *reproducible* one: no
+//! clock, no thread count and no wall time reaches the counters, so two runs with the same `n` print
+//! the same bytes in any process on any machine — which is what lets a page carry a table and a gate
+//! compare it, `lakelet/4.15-budget-regression`'s argument one level up. `--seconds` is the *honest*
+//! one: it is the run a human samples, how many cycles it got is the machine's business, and so
+//! nothing about it is reproducible and nothing may depend on it.
+//!
+//! # What this mode is not
+//!
+//! It is not a second query tool. It says nothing about `lakeletq`'s contract, it reads the store
+//! through the engine rather than over the protocol, and it opens no socket at all: its job is to
+//! *put this process in the state you want to look at*, and a profile of it is the evidence.
+
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
+
+use lakelet::Error;
+use lakelet::engine::compaction::Segments;
+use lakelet::engine::trace::Trace;
+
+/// The prefix every line of a profile carries: this program's name, the way a metric line carries
+/// the name of the thing that produced it.
+pub const PREFIX: &str = "lakelet-load";
+
+/// The code a deployment that cannot work leaves behind, whatever was asked.
+pub const BROKEN: u8 = 1;
+
+/// The code a call this program cannot answer leaves behind.
+pub const WRONG: u8 = 2;
+
+/// The code a store that refused leaves behind.
+pub const REFUSED: u8 = 4;
+
+/// Milliseconds between two samples of the workload: [`super::simulate`]'s own step, and the width
+/// one window of a cycle's question is a whole number of.
+const SAMPLE_MILLIS: i64 = 1_000;
+
+/// Everything this mode can tell a user about itself.
+pub const USAGE: &str = "\
+usage: lakelet-load --profile (--cycles <n> | --seconds <n>) [--store <dir>] [WORKLOAD OPTIONS]
+
+The profile mode runs this loader's own workload against a store of its own, in this process, so
+that a sampler has something to look at. One cycle ingests a batch through the protocol path, seals
+it into the store, and asks the store one question about the span it just wrote. What it prints is
+the phase counters `lakelet/4.15-budget-regression` established, then the workload's own shape:
+
+    lakelet-load headers=36        one line per phase, in the order a query walks them
+    lakelet-load blocks=8
+    lakelet-load decompress=8
+    lakelet-load decode=8000
+    lakelet-load filter=8000
+    lakelet-load aggregate=8000
+    lakelet-load render=8
+    lakelet-load cycles=8          the cycles the run got
+    lakelet-load records=8000      the records they ingested
+    lakelet-load bytes=232832      the frame bytes they sent
+    lakelet-load seconds=30        the bound a --seconds run was given, and nothing else
+
+OPTIONS:
+    --profile               run this mode: the other mode of this program is the default
+    --cycles <n>            stop after n cycles, at least 1. No clock is read, so two runs with
+                            the same n print the same bytes — this is the run a page may quote
+    --seconds <n>           stop after n seconds of wall clock, and run at least one cycle. This is
+                            the run a human samples: how many cycles it got is the machine's
+                            business, and the run is evidence only through what it printed
+    --store <dir>           the store to write. It is created if it is not there; with no --store
+                            a run writes to one of this process's own, which is removed afterwards
+    --samples <n>           records a cycle ingests (default 1000)
+    --batch <n>             records in one protocol frame (default 128)
+    --seed <n>              the workload's seed (default 1)
+    --series <n>            distinct series the workload spreads over (default 8)
+    --help, -h              print this help and exit
+    --version               print the version and exit
+
+Exit codes: 0 the run finished, 2 the call was wrong, 3 a question the store would not answer,
+4 the store refused, 1 this deployment is broken.
+";
+
+/// A refusal: the class it belongs to, and the sentence a reader acts on.
+///
+/// One value for both halves — a flag that cannot be read and a store that will not open — because
+/// the code and the sentence are one decision, and a caller that printed half of one and then failed
+/// is worse than a caller that printed nothing. `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s `Outcome` makes the
+/// same point about a command line, and this is that rule in the mode that added a second grammar to
+/// this program.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Refusal {
+    /// What the process would exit with.
+    code: u8,
+    /// The sentence standard error would have received.
+    detail: String,
+}
+
+impl Refusal {
+    /// The code this refusal belongs to.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn code(&self) -> u8 {
+        self.code
+    }
+
+    /// The sentence a reader acts on.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn detail(&self) -> &str {
+        &self.detail
+    }
+
+    /// A line this program cannot answer.
+    fn caller(detail: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            code: WRONG,
+            detail: detail.into(),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Something this deployment cannot do, whatever the line asked for.
+    fn broken(detail: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            code: BROKEN,
+            detail: detail.into(),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// A failure the library reported, in the class its variant is.
+    fn failed(error: Error) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            code: exit_code(&error),
+            detail: error.to_string(),
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// When a profile run stops.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Stop {
+    /// After this many cycles, at least one.
+    Cycles(u64),
+    /// After this many seconds of wall clock, and at least one cycle.
+    Seconds(u64),
+}
+
+impl Stop {
+    /// The number the line gave, whichever kind of stop it was.
+    #[must_use]
+    const fn count(self) -> u64 {
+        match self {
+            Self::Cycles(count) | Self::Seconds(count) => count,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The `seconds=` a run of this stop prints: the bound a timed run was given, and nothing for a
+    /// counted one. The bound rather than the time it took, because the time it took is a
+    /// measurement of the machine and this line is there to say *which* run a reader is looking at.
+    #[must_use]
+    const fn bound(self) -> Option<u64> {
+        match self {
+            Self::Cycles(_) => None,
+            Self::Seconds(seconds) => Some(seconds),
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one `--profile` line asked for, once the text on it is numbers and paths.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+enum Asked {
+    /// Run the workload: where to write it, with what, and when to stop.
+    Run {
+        /// The store the line named, if it named one.
+        store: Option<PathBuf>,
+        /// The workload, in the grammar the ingest mode already reads.
+        load: super::Options,
+        /// When to stop.
+        stop: Stop,
+    },
+    /// The help page.
+    Help,
+    /// The version line.
+    Version,
+}
+
+impl Asked {
+    /// Read one profile line, or answer `None` for a line that is not one.
+    ///
+    /// `None` matters: this mode is asked about *every* line this program is given, and the ingest
+    /// mode's grammar is not this one. A line without `--profile` is the other mode's, and this
+    /// function has then said nothing about it.
+    fn parse(args: impl Iterator<Item = String>) -> Result<Option<Self>, Refusal> {
+        let args: Vec<String> = args.into_iter().collect();
+        if !args.iter().any(|argument| argument == "--profile") {
+            return Ok(None);
+        }
+        // Asking for the help page is not a run that can fail, and it is answered wherever it
+        // appears — `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s rule for the other tool in this project.
+        if args
+            .iter()
+            .any(|argument| argument == "--help" || argument == "-h")
+        {
+            return Ok(Some(Self::Help));
+        }
+        if args.iter().any(|argument| argument == "--version") {
+            return Ok(Some(Self::Version));
+        }
+
+        let mut rest: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
+        let mut cycles: Option<u64> = None;
+        let mut seconds: Option<u64> = None;
+        let mut store: Option<PathBuf> = None;
+        let mut args = args.into_iter();
+        while let Some(argument) = args.next() {
+            match argument.as_str() {
+                "--profile" => {}
+                "--cycles" => cycles = Some(count(&mut args, "--cycles")?),
+                "--seconds" => seconds = Some(count(&mut args, "--seconds")?),
+                "--store" => store = Some(PathBuf::from(value(&mut args, "--store")?)),
+                other => rest.push(other.to_owned()),
+            }
+        }
+
+        let stop = match (cycles, seconds) {
+            (Some(cycles), None) => Stop::Cycles(cycles),
+            (None, Some(seconds)) => Stop::Seconds(seconds),
+            (None, None) => {
+                return Err(Refusal::caller(
+                    "`--profile` was not told when to stop: it takes `--cycles <n>` or \
+                     `--seconds <n>`, and a run that was never told is not a profile",
+                ));
+            }
+            (Some(_), Some(_)) => {
+                return Err(Refusal::caller(
+                    "`--profile` takes `--cycles` or `--seconds` and not both: they are two \
+                     answers to when this run stops",
+                ));
+            }
+        };
+
+        // What is left of the line is this program's own ingest grammar, read by the one parser that
+        // owns it: a profile run takes the workload options the ingest mode takes, with the same
+        // defaults, because it is the same workload. `--target` names the daemon the *other* mode
+        // writes to, so a profile run reads it and has nothing to do with it.
+        let load = super::Options::from_args(rest.into_iter()).map_err(Refusal::failed)?;
+        Ok(Some(Self::Run { store, load, stop }))
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one profile run did: the count of every phase, and the workload those counts were charged
+/// with.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Run {
+    /// Every cycle's query as one report ([`Trace::merge`]): the counts add, and so do the runs
+    /// behind them.
+    trace: Trace,
+    /// How many cycles the run got.
+    cycles: u64,
+    /// How many records those cycles ingested.
+    records: u64,
+    /// How many bytes of frames they sent.
+    bytes: u64,
+    /// The wall-clock bound the run was given, for a `--seconds` run.
+    seconds: Option<u64>,
+}
+
+impl Run {
+    /// A run that has done nothing yet, stopped by `stop`.
+    fn new(stop: Stop) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            trace: Trace::new(),
+            cycles: 0,
+            records: 0,
+            bytes: 0,
+            seconds: stop.bound(),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The lines one profile run prints, in the order a reader checks them: the seven phases in the
+    /// order a query walks them, then the workload's own shape.
+    ///
+    /// A phase that charged nothing is a zero on its own line rather than a missing one — "this run
+    /// decoded nothing" is a fact about the run, and a profile that left it out would make a phase
+    /// that did no work indistinguishable from a phase that was never asked
+    /// (`lakelet/4.15-budget-regression`).
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn lines(&self) -> Vec<String> {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.16-profiling: one line per phase in the order `Phase::ALL` walks them, then \
+             the workload's own shape — the format `USAGE` prints"
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+/// Run the workload against the store at `store`, until `stop` says to stop.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// A [`Refusal`] when the workload cannot be put through the protocol, when the store will not take
+/// it, and when the store will not answer the question: a failure the library reports is carried in
+/// the class [`exit_code`] gives its variant, and a refusal to answer in the class its own variant
+/// names.
+pub fn run(store: &Path, load: &super::Options, stop: Stop) -> Result<Run, Refusal> {
+    let mut segments = Segments::open(store).map_err(Refusal::failed)?;
+    let mut run = Run::new(stop);
+    // Where this run's own timestamps begin, which is the store's business rather than a counter's:
+    // see [`resume`].
+    let from = resume(&segments, step_millis(load.samples));
+
+    match stop {
+        Stop::Cycles(_) => {
+            while run.cycles < stop.count() {
+                cycle(&mut segments, load, from, &mut run)?;
+            }
+        }
+        // A timed run is stopped by the clock and never before it has done something: the check is
+        // after the cycle, so `--seconds 1` on a machine that answers in a millisecond still
+        // measures one cycle rather than none.
+        Stop::Seconds(_) => {
+            let started = Instant::now();
+            let bound = Duration::from_secs(stop.count());
+            loop {
+                cycle(&mut segments, load, from, &mut run)?;
+                if started.elapsed() >= bound {
+                    break;
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    Ok(run)
+}
+
+/// The span of one batch of `samples`: how wide a window of a cycle's question is, and how far apart
+/// two cycles' timestamps are.
+///
+/// The width is a batch rather than a unit a reader would choose, and it is chosen for the same
+/// reason the phase counts are checkable: with a window per cycle, `render` is one per cycle
+/// whatever the grid did, so the shape of the answer is a number the workload wrote down rather than
+/// one the grid decided.
+fn step_millis(samples: usize) -> i64 {
+    let batch = i64::try_from(samples).unwrap_or(i64::MAX);
+    batch.max(1).saturating_mul(SAMPLE_MILLIS)
+}
+
+/// The first timestamp of this run that no segment of the store already states, or nothing when the
+/// store is empty.
+///
+/// A run that was given a store with segments in it **appends** to it: it starts on the first whole
+/// window past the latest timestamp the manifest states, so the spans it writes cannot overlap the
+/// ones that are there. That is what makes a cycle's question meet one segment on any store rather
+/// than only on a store the run made — the conservation `decode == records` is about the store, not
+/// about the loop's counter of its own cycles.
+fn resume(segments: &Segments, step: i64) -> Option<i64> {
+    let latest = segments
+        .manifest()
+        .segments()
+        .iter()
+        .filter_map(|info| info.last_millis())
+        .max()?;
+    let slot = i64::try_from(latest).unwrap_or(i64::MAX) / step.max(1) + 1;
+    Some(slot.saturating_mul(step.max(1)))
+}
+
+/// One cycle: the workload, the protocol path, the flush, and the question about what was flushed.
+///
+/// The trace is charged here rather than derived afterwards: `outcome.trace()` is the executor's own
+/// account of the run it just did, and the first cycle's is *the* report while every later one is
+/// merged into it (`Trace::merge`), so `runs` says how many cycles the profile covers.
+fn cycle(
+    segments: &mut Segments,
+    load: &super::Options,
+    from: Option<i64>,
+    run: &mut Run,
+) -> Result<(), Refusal> {
+    let _ = (segments, load, from, run);
+    todo!(
+        "lakelet/4.16-profiling: the workload with its timestamps moved on to `from`, the frames a \
+         client would send read back out of their own bytes, `Segments::seal`, and then one \
+         question about the span this cycle wrote — answered by `exec::execute` and added to the \
+         run's `Trace`"
+    )
+}
+
+/// The code this program leaves behind for `error`: the class the variant is, decided in one place.
+///
+/// Both modes of this binary answer with this rule, because one program answers a failure one way
+/// whatever it was asked to do: `2` is the caller's, `4` is the store's, and everything else — a
+/// configuration this build cannot read — is a deployment that is broken rather than a line that is
+/// wrong.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn exit_code(error: &Error) -> u8 {
+    match error {
+        Error::Invalid { .. } => WRONG,
+        Error::Protocol { .. } | Error::Store { .. } => REFUSED,
+        Error::Config { .. } => BROKEN,
+        _ => BROKEN,
+    }
+}
+
+/// Run the profile mode, if this line asked for it.
+///
+/// `None` when the line is not a profile line: the ingest mode is this program's then, and nothing
+/// here has said anything about it. `Some(code)` when it was: everything this mode prints has been
+/// printed, and the code is what the process leaves behind.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn main(args: impl Iterator<Item = String>) -> Option<u8> {
+    let asked = match Asked::parse(args) {
+        Ok(None) => return None,
+        Ok(Some(asked)) => asked,
+        Err(refusal) => return Some(complained(&refusal)),
+    };
+    let (store, load, stop) = match asked {
+        Asked::Help => {
+            println!("{USAGE}");
+            return Some(0);
+        }
+        Asked::Version => {
+            println!("{PREFIX} {}", env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION"));
+            return Some(0);
+        }
+        Asked::Run { store, load, stop } => (store, load, stop),
+    };
+
+    // The two ways a path can be wrong are told apart *before* a store is opened, which is what makes
+    // the codes mean the same thing however the line was written (`lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s rule
+    // for the read side of this project). A directory that is not there is not one of them: this mode
+    // writes, so it creates it.
+    let owned = match Store::of(store.as_deref()) {
+        Ok(owned) => owned,
+        Err(refusal) => return Some(complained(&refusal)),
+    };
+    match run(owned.path(), &load, stop) {
+        Ok(run) => {
+            for line in run.lines() {
+                println!("{line}");
+            }
+            Some(0)
+        }
+        Err(refusal) => Some(complained(&refusal)),
+    }
+}
+
+/// Report a refusal on standard error and answer the code it belongs to.
+///
+/// Nothing of a refused run reaches standard output: a script that pipes a profile into a parser must
+/// never be handed half a profile beside a complaint.
+fn complained(refusal: &Refusal) -> u8 {
+    eprintln!("{PREFIX}: {}", refusal.detail());
+    refusal.code()
+}
+
+/// The store a profile run writes to.
+struct Store {
+    /// Where it is.
+    path: PathBuf,
+    /// Whether this process made it, and so owes the filesystem a `remove_dir_all`.
+    temporary: bool,
+}
+
+impl Store {
+    /// The directory `named` names, or one of this process's own.
+    fn of(named: Option<&Path>) -> Result<Self, Refusal> {
+        let Some(named) = named else {
+            let path =
+                std::env::temp_dir().join(format!("{PREFIX}-profile-{}", std::process::id()));
+            // A run's counts are counts of the store it wrote, so a store left behind by a run with
+            // this process's id would be counted too. It is emptied rather than reused, which is what
+            // makes the numbers a reader sees the numbers of a fresh store — the whole reason a
+            // `--cycles` table can be quoted on a page.
+            let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&path);
+            return Ok(Self {
+                path,
+                temporary: true,
+            });
+        };
+        match std::fs::metadata(named) {
+            Ok(metadata) if !metadata.is_dir() => Err(Refusal::broken(format!(
+                "`{}` is not a directory, so this deployment cannot be a store: nothing about the \
+                 call is wrong, and nothing can be written either",
+                named.display()
+            ))),
+            _ => Ok(Self {
+                path: named.to_path_buf(),
+                temporary: false,
+            }),
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Where the store is.
+    fn path(&self) -> &Path {
+        &self.path
+    }
+}
+
+impl Drop for Store {
+    fn drop(&mut self) {
+        if self.temporary {
+            let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&self.path);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// The value of a flag that needs one.
+fn value(args: &mut impl Iterator<Item = String>, flag: &str) -> Result<String, Refusal> {
+    args.next().ok_or_else(|| {
+        Refusal::caller(format!(
+            "`{flag}` needs a value: a profile run is stopped by `{flag} <n>`"
+        ))
+    })
+}
+
+/// The count a stop needs: at least one, because a run that would do nothing is not a measurement.
+fn count(args: &mut impl Iterator<Item = String>, flag: &str) -> Result<u64, Refusal> {
+    let text = value(args, flag)?;
+    let parsed: u64 = text.parse().map_err(|_| {
+        Refusal::caller(format!("`{flag}` takes a number, and `{text}` is not one"))
+    })?;
+    if parsed == 0 {
+        return Err(Refusal::caller(format!(
+            "`{flag} 0` would be a run that did nothing at all, so its profile would be seven \
+             zeroes: give it at least one"
+        )));
+    }
+    Ok(parsed)
+}
